  "text": "None of the three solutions gives the threshold I believe is correct, which I recall as 1+√2 ≈ 2.414 from Fey, Levine and Peres, \"Growth rates and explosions in sandpiles\" (2010). I'm working from memory and did not verify it with a search.\n\n- **Solution 1 (≈ 2.6067):** The claimed value 1 + Σ 1/(2ⁿ−1) is an Erdős–Borwein-type sum. The \"back-pressure\" recursion it relies on is asserted, not derived, so this doesn't match the known result.\n- **Solution 2 (q = 3):** Its sharpness construction is only sketched, and the stated upper bound (mass M reaches at most ~M³ sources) is not justified. It disagrees with the known threshold.\n- **Solution 3 (≈ 1.6931):** The \"path-independent integral\" ∫₂^∞ dg/(g(g−1)) = ln 2 is an unsupported optimization over architectures. It also has the explosion threshold below the comb's 4/3 example is not contradicted here, but it still does not match the known value.\n\nSince none of the three matches 1+√2, none is correct.\n\nCHOICE: NONE",
